Falsifies `system_tests::user::x86_port_tests::a_revoked_holder_faults_on_its_next_port_write`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect is `PortRange::REVOKE` doing only the half that every other revoke does: deleting the
capability from every other holder's table, and not the half this object alone needs, which is
forgetting the cached grant (`Thread::port_range_grant`) the context switch installs from. With
the six lines that clear the cache removed from `sched::delete_port_range_caps_impl`, the revoked
holder's capability is gone from its table and its grant survives; when the test wakes it, its
switch-in installs the range into the TSS and its `out` is permitted.

It is the same class of defect milestone 313's audit found live in the tree on the self-delete
path (`sched::delete_current_cap`): the table and the cache are two records of one authority, and
a path that updates one and not the other is enforcement that outlives the capability. This record
proves the revoke test is wired to the cache half rather than to the table half, which is the half
that matters, since nothing on the port path ever reads the table.

Before milestone 313's audit the test could not have gone red for this: the revoked holder reported
after its `out` on a rendezvous nobody was receiving, so a surviving grant hung the run.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(msg[0], EVENT_FAULT, "a revoked holder's next `out` must fault; the
word must never arrive")`, holding `EVENT_EXIT` (2) where it wanted `EVENT_FAULT` (1).

Regenerated 2026-09-21 (falsif lane) against the context PR #1059 (risk 7's adversarial pass) moved:
that pull request inserted the `outgoing_cap` hand-off sweep directly above the six lines this patch
removes, so the old hunk no longer matched. Same six lines, same defect, new surrounding context.

Regenerated again 2026-09-25 (UTC) against 53cb6d620, which landed
milestone 315 (a port revoke that reaches every core). That commit replaced the single-core TSS reset below these six lines with a
broadcast inside the lock. The broadcast does not rescue the defect: it resets every core's bitmap
once, and the revoked holder's next switch-in reinstalls the grant from the cache this patch leaves
standing.

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/capabilities.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

